{"schema_version":1,"id":"019e8ff1-0d40-72df-bb5c-448f799bcb7b","stream":{"kind":"exec.usage","id":"01a1a0c6-e14d-79a1-9f3c-a04949fbc875"},"sequence":1,"recorded_at":1780531400000678,"record_type":"status","durability":"ephemeral","causation_id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3","payload_type":"run.usage","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_usage","command_id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3"},"models":[{"model":"muse-wonder-1.0","input_tokens":22655,"cached_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2670,"reasoning_tokens":1369,"calls":1}],"calls":1,"completed_calls":1,"failed_calls":0,"cancelled_calls":0,"children_included":false,"allowance":{"tier":null,"window":{"used_percent":46,"resets_at_ms":1793698907000},"weekly":{"used_percent":18,"resets_at_ms":1794182400000},"as_of_ms":1793698779805,"source":"stream"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c5f6","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a1a0c6-e14d-79a1-9f3c-a04949fbc875"},"sequence":341,"recorded_at":1780531400000678,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3","payload_type":"run.terminal.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"run_terminal","command_id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"01a1a0c6-e5b9-72d2-a84b-96a08eea60a3"},"terminal":"completed","text":"Advisory review only — no files changed.\n\n**Verdict:** The revised plan is directionally right: reuse the canonical Rust kernel, do not build a second one. But a Wasm-consuming Desktop prototype is **not dependency-ready under your stated rules**. Canonical package delivery comes first. Today you can only build preparatory scaffolding (an internal port with the Pierre adapter, a comparison corpus, instrumentation) that imports nothing from AX Code.\n\n**Why delivery blocks it**\n- [`package.json`](/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop) (as supplied) has `\"private\": true`, depends on `@ax-code/util: workspace:*`, and `dist/`+`wasm/` are git-ignored build outputs. Nothing independently installable exists.\n- Your rules forbid sibling-source and ignored-`dist` imports and duplicate runtimes — precisely the only three ways to consume it today.\n- Required first deliverable from the owner: a pinned, installable artifact (registry package or versioned tarball with integrity hash) with `workspace:*` removed or bundled, a `prepack` build, the `NOTICE` (BSD-3-Clause jsdiff), and a frozen `version: 1` protocol. Also confirm the worker asset story: [`browser.ts`](/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop) loads `./browser-worker.js` via `new URL(..., import.meta.url)`, which must survive Desktop's bundler/packager, plus `application/wasm` MIME and CSP Wasm permission in the packaged app, not just dev.\n\n**API-to-UI mapping issues**\n- Desktop is synchronous everywhere: `getFileHunks()` feeds `useMemo` in [DiffHunkReviewList.tsx](/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop:62) and an effect in [usePublishReviewHunks.ts](/Users/akiralam/code/ax-code-desktop:30). The engine is async, **one in-flight call per engine, concurrent calls reject without queueing**, and abort terminates the worker. Many files mounting at once will collide; you need a single-flight scheduler with coalescing/debounce, a content-hash cache (existing 64-entry hash cache is a good seed), and caller-side owner/revision tokens — the API's request IDs are internal and do not discard stale *UI* results for you.\n- Cancelling by worker termination makes per-keystroke cancel expensive (Wasm recompile on recreate). Cancel only superseded *queued* work eagerly; terminate in-flight work sparingly.\n- Pierre still parses internally to render. Until the renderer consumes canonical hunks, a \"shared snapshot\" only unifies list/navigation/revert — rendering stays a second parse. Shadow-mode comparison is the honest prototype scope.\n- Field mapping needs the unpublished `protocol.ts`: Pierre rows use `hunkSpecs`, `additionLines`/`deletionLines` counts, `additionStart`. Canonical hunks are one-based **including empty ranges** with fixed 4-line context — insertion-at-EOF and pure-deletion empty ranges are the edge cases most likely to misalign headers, counts, and focus anchors.\n- Caps (16 MiB/payload, 4,096 hunks, 200,000 lines) are below \"whole file\" assumptions; files Desktop currently renders via Pierre beyond those caps need an explicit Pierre fallback or a defined placeholder path, not a rejection surfaced as \"no hunks.\"\n\n**Subset/reverse-apply correctness — the hard part**\n- `applyHunks` is forward-only. Desktop's revert is the inverse: take `modified`, undo one hunk. The README's subset note (\"rebasing its `newStart`\") covers forward subsets on the *original*, not reverse application. You must construct swapped hunks and rebase them in modified-side coordinates (apply bottom-up or recompute offsets), then prove: strict context matching still succeeds when neighbouring hunks' 4-line contexts overlap the reverted region; CRLF files use raw mode only (`normalize: true` hunks applied to raw CRLF source will correctly-but-uselessly reject); missing-final-newline round-trips byte-exactly.\n- Segmentation risk is real: if the Rust kernel segments differently from Pierre/jsdiff, hunk *indices* shift, focus anchors drift, and Git staging — matched today only by `candidate.newStart === hunk.additionStart` — silently loses actions or, worse, matches a same-start/different-extent hunk. Strengthen identity to `(oldStart, oldLines, newStart, newLines)` plus content hash before any migration, and treat \"no match\" (current safe behaviour) as the only acceptable mismatch outcome.\n- Keep the host-side guard you already planned: verify the on-disk snapshot hash immediately before writing a revert, since `applyHunks`' atomic rejection only validates against the string you pass it.\n\n**Measurements required before replacing Pierre** (none exist yet; do not quote any)\n1. Segmentation agreement on a real corpus (CRLF, Unicode/emoji, no-final-newline, empty-side, Markdown block cases from your completed fix, large files near caps): % files with identical hunk boundaries.\n2. Byte-exact round-trips: full apply, forward subsets, single-hunk reverse, across that corpus — zero-diff requirement.\n3. Staging-match rate vs. current Pierre behaviour on real `git diff` output.\n4. Latency: cold worker start + Wasm compile, warm `diff` p50/p95 by file size, termination/recreate cost; UI long-task impact vs. today's synchronous parse.\n5. Rejection/fallback behaviour at each cap.\n\n**Sequencing:** (1) owner ships the pinned artifact; (2) shadow read-only worker preview comparing against Pierre on the corpus; (3) migrate revert to reverse-apply behind snapshot checks once (2) shows boundary parity; (4) staging last, only after identity matching is proven.","reason":null}}
